  "summary_md": "The medical instruments and supplies sector today presents a sharp divide between companies converting regulatory and product-cycle wins into commercial traction and those betting on strategic pivots to reverse pressure. KRMD and ANGO stand out with durable growth and credible profitability paths, while POCI scaled manufacturing to its first positive adjusted EBITDA. STXS begins early commercial use of its long-awaited robotic system, and OSUR’s mid-2026 FDA decisions could be transformative. MBOT’s first revenue is clouded by surgeon pushback, making its adoption story especially live. BNGO and FEMY are building clinical evidence and commercial foundations, though cash runway and reimbursement remain watchpoints.\n\nConversely, AZTA’s guidance reversal and fraud inquiry, EMBC’s U.S. share loss and dividend cut alongside its Owen Mumford acquisition, and PDEX’s core miss masked by a one-time gain signal serious execution risks. NXGL’s going-concern pivot via acquisition and HBIO’s restructuring underscore a batch-wide pattern of M&A and operational resets. Reimbursement friction (EKSO, MLSS) and tight cash runways recur. The overriding question is whether recent regulatory greenlights and bold deals can overcome credibility gaps and mixed demand.",
